ed he was  a member of that team and I think  there's a real conflict of interest so I  think it would be in the best interest  the investigation going forward if  somewhere else on the committee were to  lead it but we really need to get this  back on track and I think the majority  can start out by rescheduling this here  and we've urged them to do that but we  have yet to hear back congressman let's  talk about the substance about perhaps  he wanted to hear from Sally Yates she  was the acting Attorney General she  informed the White House Council that  their National Security Advisor  Michael Flynn had made comments that  were perhaps untruthful what did you  want to hear directly from her well I  certainly wanted to hear the events that  led up to Michael Flynn's firing and  what has concerned me about this for  some time is according to newspaper  accounts she brought information to the  White House that indicated that Mike  Flynn had lied about a conversation he  had with a Russian ambassador and not  just about any topic this was on this  subject apparently at least according to  reports of the sanctions that President  Obama imposed on Russia over Russia's  interference in the election to help  Donald Trump so that's obviously a very  significant conversation it led to his  firing when he misled the vice president  and the vice president misled the  country but you have a period of some  time it looks like weeks in which the  president was aware that Michael Flint  had lied and the Vice President had  misrepresent the country misrepresented  the events to the country and the  president did nothing about it it was  only a White House Council didn't inform  the president of that conversation with  the acting Attorney General I would  certainly like to know I have to expect  that the White House Counsel did inform  the president and the question is why  didn't the president act why was the  president content to have the country  mislead about this secret conve